 "experiments": [
  {
   "title": "Timestamps: four ways to hand Arrow dates to plotly.js",
   "description": "Browser only (no server, no network): decode the payload, build traces, `Plotly.react`, paint. Same Arrow payload for every Arrow mode; the conversions are in [web/dates.js](web/dates.js). *toISOString*: one Date and string per value. *Fast ISO strings*: the same strings with the date part made once per day and the time part once per second of the day. *Date objects*: what I use today when passing Arrow data to plotly.js by hand. *UTC ms, patched plotly.js*: a Float64Array that plotly.js takes as is after the one-condition change in [patches/optimisations/plotly-date-typed-array.patch](patches/optimisations/plotly-date-typed-array.patch). The last two columns drop the timestamp column (row index on a linear axis) to show what dates cost. The browser runs in UTC so all modes with dates draw the same axis ranges, which is checked.",
   "notes": "Date objects are shown in the browser's local time, ISO strings and patched UTC ms as written; only the first suits a chart that must follow the viewer's timezone. Patched plotly.js reads numbers on a date axis differently from today (local-time ms), so upstream it would need to be opt-in.\n\n*Tried, not adopted:* making the UTC ms on the server (casting the timestamp column to float64) saved only a few percent over converting in the browser. It also moves an O(n) cast to the server, and the payload no longer carries the Arrow timestamp type.",
   "baseline": "json",
