   "description": "Dash end to end, like bench.py: request sent to plot painted, no compression, forecast workload (half-hourly float64 with nulls, stacked SVG `scatter`). The DataFrame index is in Europe/London (from `tz_convert`), either **tz-aware** or with the zone dropped (`df.index.tz_convert(\"Europe/London\").tz_localize(None)`), served by [microbench/dash_tz_index.py](microbench/dash_tz_index.py). plotly.py stores a tz-aware index as an object array of `pd.Timestamp` in every trace, and every encoder then walks it in Python. Both draw the same chart (plotly.js shows a date string's wall-clock time and ignores its offset), which is checked. Baseline: tz-aware index with stock Dash (orjson). *arrow-ms* uses the patched plotly.js.",
   "notes": "*Side finding, optional and out of scope for the transport work:* an app change, no Dash or plotly.js change. Dropping the zone draws the same chart and helps every transport. The main benchmarks keep the tz-aware index. The end-to-end forecast benchmark (section 3) uses a UTC index, which plotly.py converts faster than a London one, so its Dash *Build* and JSON *Encode* times understate this cost.",
