   "id": "compression-estimate",
   "title": "Estimate: zstd or brotli instead of gzip level 1",
   "description": "An estimate, not a benchmark run. *No compression* and *gzip level 1* are the measured end-to-end totals from sections 2 and 3. *zstd level 1* and *brotli quality 1* are the measured gzip level 1 total with its server compression time replaced by theirs, measured here on the same response body; the browser's decompression time is assumed the same (both usually decompress at least as fast as gzip). *Break-even* is the link speed below which compressing saves more transfer time than it costs: bytes saved divided by compression time. Browsers: Chrome and Edge accept `Content-Encoding: zstd` from version 123 and Firefox from 126; the Chromium used here sends `Accept-Encoding: gzip, deflate, br, zstd`, also to localhost. brotli is accepted by every current browser over HTTPS and on localhost. A browser that does not offer an encoding still gets gzip.",
   "notes": "Over loopback, zstd level 1 would cost 0-10% and brotli quality 1 0-11%, against up to 19% for gzip level 1. The link speed below which compressing pays off rises from 0.4-1.5 Gbit/s (gzip level 1) to 0.7-4.4 Gbit/s (brotli quality 1) and 1.3-6.7 Gbit/s (zstd level 1) for JSON, and from 20-320 Mbit/s to 0.15-1.2 Gbit/s and 0.5-2.9 Gbit/s for Arrow, so either pays off on almost any network. Against gzip level 1, JSON comes out 8-31% smaller with zstd and 7-26% smaller with brotli. Arrow comes out within about 20% either way (random float32 values hardly compress): zstd makes Dash's scale responses up to 20% larger, brotli up to 11% smaller. So compressed JSON can be smaller than compressed Arrow: Arrow's gains are in CPU time, not bytes. Serving zstd or brotli needs server support: zstd is in the Python 3.14+ standard library and brotli in the `brotli` package, Starlette's GZipMiddleware does gzip only, and Flask-Compress support depends on its version.",
